# Node / seed independence (IID) summary

Per design cell (243) × metric diagnostics on 1D-normalized ratios (`f_ratio`, `abs_TF_ratio`, `PGA_ratio`, `PSA_ratio`, `Ia_ratio`) from `join_master.h5`.

- Node spacing: **2.0 m**; Ljung–Box lag = 10; α = 0.05
- Primary scale: **ln(ratio)**; raw lag-1 reported for Box parity
- Independence gate (nodes / seed labels): median abs lag-1 < 0.2 and Ljung–Box reject fraction < 0.2

## Aggregate across design cells

### Node dependence and identical distribution

| Metric | med node lag1 (ln) | frac cells with node LB reject ≥ 50% | frac cells KW reject | frac cells nodes independent | frac cells nodes iid |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| f_ratio | 0.966 | 1.000 | 0.008 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| abs_TF_ratio | 0.999 | 1.000 | 0.189 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| PGA_ratio | 0.991 | 1.000 | 0.420 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| PSA_ratio | 0.995 | 1.000 | 0.395 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| Ia_ratio | 0.994 | 1.000 | 0.498 | 0.000 | 0.000 |

### Seed diagnostics (fixed-node series vs shared seed effect)

| Metric | med seed abs lag1 (ln) | med seed LB reject frac | frac cells seed labels exchangeable | med seed ICC | med pairwise node corr | frac cells abs ICC < 0.05 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| f_ratio | 0.065 | 0.000 | 0.905 | 0.804 | 0.811 | 0.000 |
| abs_TF_ratio | 0.051 | 0.000 | 0.728 | 0.886 | 0.884 | 0.000 |
| PGA_ratio | 0.063 | 0.030 | 0.979 | 0.167 | 0.167 | 0.119 |
| PSA_ratio | 0.054 | 0.000 | 0.963 | 0.188 | 0.190 | 0.099 |
| Ia_ratio | 0.060 | 0.020 | 0.996 | 0.174 | 0.174 | 0.078 |

## Conclusions

1. **Are nodes independent of each other?** **No.** Median node lag-1 correlation (ln) across cells/metrics is **0.994**; only 0.0% of cell×metric cases meet the independence gate. Strong spatial serial dependence along the 2 m node array matches prior Box `channel_iid` findings.

2. **Within each design cell, are nodes iid?** **No.** IID requires independence *and* identical distributions. Only 0.0% of cell×metric cases pass both gates. Lag-1 dependence alone rules out iid even when marginals look similar (Kruskal–Wallis rejects equal node distributions in 30.2% of cell×metric cases overall; rejection is metric-dependent).

3. **Within each design cell, are seeds iid?** **Yes at a fixed node; no for the joint (node × seed) sample.**

#### Clarification: fixed node vs joint array

| Scope | Treat as iid? | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Seeds at **one fixed node** in one cell | **Yes** | seed-index median abs lag-1 = **0.059**; median LB reject fraction = **0.000**; **91.4%** of cell×metric cases pass the exchangeability gate |
| Nodes along the array (same seed) | **No** | median node lag-1 = **0.994** |
| Joint (node × seed) as if all draws were iid | **No** | median seed ICC = **0.275**; median pairwise node corr = **0.278**; only 5.9% of cases have abs(ICC) < 0.05 |

The large seed ICC means: *given the same seed*, nodes move together (shared random-field realization). It does **not** mean seed 3 and seed 17 at a fixed node are dependent — those are separate Monte Carlo draws. Overall, seed labels look exchangeable in **91.4%** of cell×metric cases, with overall median abs seed lag-1 **0.059**.

### Per-metric notes

- **`f_ratio`**: med node lag1=0.966; med seed abs lag1=0.065; med seed ICC=0.804; nodes_iid in 0.0% of cells; seed labels exchangeable in 90.5% of cells.
- **`abs_TF_ratio`**: med node lag1=0.999; med seed abs lag1=0.051; med seed ICC=0.886; nodes_iid in 0.0% of cells; seed labels exchangeable in 72.8% of cells.
- **`PGA_ratio`**: med node lag1=0.991; med seed abs lag1=0.063; med seed ICC=0.167; nodes_iid in 0.0% of cells; seed labels exchangeable in 97.9% of cells.
- **`PSA_ratio`**: med node lag1=0.995; med seed abs lag1=0.054; med seed ICC=0.188; nodes_iid in 0.0% of cells; seed labels exchangeable in 96.3% of cells.
- **`Ia_ratio`**: med node lag1=0.994; med seed abs lag1=0.060; med seed ICC=0.174; nodes_iid in 0.0% of cells; seed labels exchangeable in 99.6% of cells.

### Modeling implication

- **Fixed node** (e.g. center recorder): seed averages and SEs under an iid-seed model are appropriate.
- **Across nodes** within a cell: do **not** assume nodes are iid. Prefer spatial models (semivariogram / correlation length).
- **Pooling node × seed**: do **not** treat all cells of the matrix as iid; use seed clustering or mixed effects when the same seed couples nodes.

